      "narrative": "Pima County’s residential market is showing slight downward price pressure, with recent local reporting saying the median list price eased to $400,000 and Redfin showing Tucson home prices down 1.4% year over year over the three months ending June 2026.[1][11] Inventory is not surging but remains elevated enough to slow sales, with one county update reporting 3,154 active homes and Redfin saying homes in Pima County are taking 73 days to sell and receiving 2 offers on average.[1][2] The market still tilts slightly toward sellers, but only modestly, as local reporting describes a slight seller’s advantage while also noting more price reductions and longer days on market.[1] Local drivers are mixed: county housing officials point to migration of working-age remote workers, competition from institutional investors, and a recent slowdown in single-family permit activity, while also noting that existing home sales rose slightly and median resale prices stayed level in FY 2025/26.[14]",
